prompt.text:
Repair the following complete `solution.rs` while preserving its API. `Canvas::new` requires exactly `width * height * 4` bytes, reporting overflow or a mismatched size as an error. `put_pixel` accepts only coordinates strictly inside the canvas, never panics, and leaves the buffer unchanged on error. RGBA stores bytes as red, green, blue, alpha; BGRA swaps red and blue. Zero-sized canvases are permitted but no pixel can be drawn.

Known-bad source:

#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum PixelOrder { Rgba, Bgra }

#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Canvas {
    pub width: usize,
    pub height: usize,
    pub order: PixelOrder,
    pixels: Vec<u8>,
}

impl Canvas {
    pub fn new(width: usize, height: usize, order: PixelOrder, pixels: Vec<u8>) -> Result<Self, String> {
        let expected = width.checked_mul(height).and_then(|n| n.checked_mul(4))
            .ok_or_else(|| "canvas size overflow".to_string())?;
        if pixels.len() != expected { return Err("pixel buffer has the wrong size".to_string()); }
        Ok(Self { width, height, order, pixels })
    }

    pub fn pixels(&self) -> &[u8] { &self.pixels }

    pub fn put_pixel(&mut self, x: usize, y: usize, color: [u8; 4]) -> Result<(), String> {
        if x > self.width || y > self.height { return Err("coordinate out of bounds".to_string()); }
        let index = (y * self.width + x) * 4;
        let stored = match self.order {
            PixelOrder::Rgba => color,
            PixelOrder::Bgra => [color[2], color[1], color[0], color[3]],
        };
        self.pixels[index..index + 4].copy_from_slice(&stored);
        Ok(())
    }
}

